From 94ac297f5ee1b5aff4351ab5731afd0b224994ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hyunjun1234 <plab2090@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 16:39:26 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 23/23] --max-turns: let the interactive session set its own
 cap
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The REPL caps each prompt at 50 turns. `--max-turns` was parsed, documented as
"(only works with --print)", and hidden from help — and in a TUI session it was
silently dropped: sessionConfig, which launchRepl spreads into <REPL/>, simply had
no maxTurns key, so resolveReplMaxTurns always saw undefined and returned 50. A
user who typed `--max-turns 200` got no error and no effect.

REPL and the resume selector already accept the prop; only the hand-off from
options was missing. That is the one line here.

Hitting the cap is a pause, not a failure — the warning is UI-only, history stays
intact, and the next message starts a fresh count — so this is about how long a
session runs unattended before it wants a human, not about capability. That is
worth putting in the user's hands rather than in a constant.

Also stop honouring a cap that cannot be meant: the flag is parsed with bare
`Number` and never validated, so `--max-turns 0` (or a typo parsing to NaN) would
end the turn before the first tool call. Zero reads as "no limit" to whoever types
it and is useless to everyone else, so both readings fall back to the default.

Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Na71jMPNwVCJecN1VMjJbo
